CAT = "cehrsNonClinical"; SEG = "NCL"
Q = [
('Before entering a new blood pressure reading, an EHR specialist opens a chart for Maria Lopez. What should be confirmed first?',
 'Name and date of birth', ['Name and room number', 'Name and the visit provider', 'Name and appointment time'],
 'Two patient identifiers, typically full name and date of birth, confirm the right chart before anything is recorded. A room number, provider or appointment time can match more than one patient.'),
("Which identifier does a facility assign to a patient to tie together all of that patient's records in its system?",
 'Medical record number', ['National Provider Identifier', 'Claim control number', 'Group policy number'],
 "The medical record number (MRN) is the facility's unique patient identifier. An NPI identifies a provider, not a patient."),
('A registration clerk searches for a returning patient by last name only and finds nothing. What should be done before creating a new record?',
 'Search again by date of birth and partial name', ['Create a new record and flag it for a later merge', 'Search again by first name only', 'Ask registration to try again tomorrow'],
 "Searching several ways (date of birth, partial or alternate spellings, phone number) prevents a duplicate MRN. A new record made too quickly splits the patient's history."),
('Which search technique best finds a patient whose last name may be spelled Kristensen or Christensen?',
 'A sound-alike or wildcard name search', ['An exact-match last name search', 'A search by first name only', 'A search limited to patients seen this year'],
 'Phonetic (sound-alike) and wildcard searches catch spelling variants of a name, a common cause of duplicate records.'),
("A patient's employer, emergency contact and preferred language are being added at check-in. Which category of EHR information is this?",
 'Demographic information', ['Clinical history data', 'Charge capture details', 'Order entry'],
 'Contact details, employer, language and similar identifying facts are demographics. Clinical data covers findings, diagnoses and treatment.'),
("A 15-year-old is seen at a clinic, and her father's insurance will be billed. In the EHR, the father is recorded as the:",
 'Guarantor', ["Subscriber's dependent", 'Attending provider', 'Emergency contact'],
 "The guarantor is the person financially responsible for the account. The minor is the patient and the dependent on the father's plan."),
("Which document, printed at admission, summarizes a patient's demographics, insurance and admitting information on one page?",
 'Face sheet', ['Operative report', 'Discharge summary', 'Progress note'],
 'The face sheet (admission sheet) gives a one-page summary of who the patient is, how they are covered and why they were admitted.'),
('A patient is admitted and needs an identification band. What should be printed from the EHR?',
 'An armband with her identifiers', ['A specimen label with her name', 'A face sheet for the chart', 'A visit label showing her MRN only'],
 'Armbands and specimen labels are generated from the EHR so the identifiers match the record exactly. Handwritten or blank bands invite identification errors.'),
('A new cardiologist joins the practice. Where should her NPI and specialty be entered so they flow into orders and claims?',
 'The provider database', ['The patient insurance screen', 'The referral tracking log', 'The user access list'],
 'Provider details are kept in the provider database (provider master file), which feeds orders, referrals and claims across patient charts. A user access list controls logins, not provider identifiers.'),
('How many positions is a National Provider Identifier?',
 '10', ['9', '8', '12'],
 'Under 45 CFR 162.406 the NPI is a 10-position numeric identifier whose last position is a check digit.'),
('What information about a provider is built into the digits of the NPI?',
 'Nothing; the number has no meaning', ["The provider's state of license", "The provider's specialty code", 'The year the provider first enrolled'],
 'The NPI carries no information about the provider: HHS describes it as intelligence-free, so moving or changing specialty does not change the number.'),
('Results from an outside laboratory arrive through an interface into the EHR. What should the EHR specialist check before they are filed to a chart?',
 'That the patient identifiers match', ['That the ordering provider has reviewed it', 'That the values are within range', 'That a paper copy also arrived'],
 "Incoming results must match the patient's identifiers before filing; a mismatched result filed to the wrong chart is a safety risk. Interpreting the values is the provider's job."),
('A specialist faxes a consult letter that is scanned into the EHR. What step keeps it findable later?',
 'Indexing it to the document type', ['Saving it under the date received', 'Attaching it to the next visit note', 'Filing it in the provider inbox only'],
 'Indexing assigns the scan to the correct patient, document type and date so it appears in the right section of the chart.'),
('After scanning a three-page referral, what should be checked before discarding or returning the paper?',
 'That every page is legible and present', ['That the file is under the upload size limit', 'That the scan is saved in color', "That the sender's fax number shows"],
 'A quality check confirms all pages scanned clearly and attached to the right patient before the paper source is handled per policy.'),
('A patient signs a consent form on an e-signature pad, but the signature does not appear in the record. What is a sensible first check?',
 "That the pad's cable is connected", ['That the form was printed first', 'That the patient pressed harder', 'That the EHR user license is still current'],
 'Basic troubleshooting starts with connections: a loose or unplugged device is a common reason captured data does not reach the EHR.'),
('Why should a clinic keep a current inventory list of its tablets, cameras and signature pads?',
 'Lost devices holding PHI can be traced', ['It is required on every claim', 'It sets the warranty dates for each device', "It decides each user's EHR role"],
 'An accurate hardware inventory (with asset tags and serial numbers) shows where every device is, so a missing device that may hold PHI is noticed and handled quickly.'),
('An inventory check finds a tablet listed in exam room 3 that is not there. What does this most likely mean?',
 'The device may be lost or moved', ['The device was retired correctly', 'The inventory list is complete', 'The tablet is no longer needed'],
 'An inaccurate list hides a lost or moved device. The gap should be investigated and reported, since mobile devices can hold or reach PHI.'),
('What detail best identifies one specific signature pad on an inventory list?',
 'Its serial number or asset tag', ['Its make and model', 'The exam room it is assigned to', 'The date it was purchased'],
 'A serial number or asset tag is unique to one device; model or color is shared by many.'),
('A patient arrives for a 10:00 visit. In patient flow, which step usually comes right after confirming the appointment?',
 'Verifying demographics and insurance', ['Rooming the patient and taking vitals', 'Printing the visit summary', 'Posting the visit charges'],
 'Check-in confirms the visit, then verifies demographics and insurance. Charges, the visit summary and the claim come at or after check-out.'),
('A provider blocks Friday afternoons for hospital rounds. Where is that set so no visits are booked then?',
 "The provider's schedule template", ['The appointment reminder call list', 'The provider database', 'The patient recall list'],
 "Schedule templates hold each provider's available hours, blocked times and slot lengths."),
("At check-out, a patient pays her coinsurance by card. What should happen to the card's security code afterward?",
 'It should not be stored', ['It should be stored encrypted', 'It should be kept until the charge clears', 'It should be saved for repeat visits'],
 "Card security codes are never kept after the payment is authorized, not even encrypted, under the card industry's data security standard. Saving them anywhere exposes the patient to fraud."),
('Which is an acceptable form of photo identification at registration?',
 "A state-issued driver's license", ['A utility bill with her name', 'An insurance card without a photo', 'A signed letter from her employer'],
 "Government-issued photo identification, such as a driver's license or passport, confirms who the patient is. A utility bill can confirm an address but not identity."),
('A patient needs to see a specialist in another practice. What does the EHR referral workflow usually record?',
 'The specialist, reason and status', ['The specialist and visit fee', "The reason and the referring clerk's name", "The specialist's office hours"],
 'A referral entry tracks who the patient is sent to, why, and whether it was sent, scheduled and completed, so nothing is lost between practices.'),
('A new receptionist starts Monday. What should happen before she is given access to the live EHR?',
 'She completes role-based EHR training', ['She shadows a coworker for a week', "She signs the vendor's software license terms", 'She reads the full user manual'],
 'New users are trained on the functions of their role before access is granted, and the training is documented.'),
('Why do many organizations give trainees a separate practice copy of the EHR?',
 'So they can practice safely', ['So training costs no license fee', 'So the live system stays faster', "So real patients' data is easier to find"],
 'A training (sandbox) environment uses test patients, so new users can practice tasks without altering real records.'),
('Which record shows that a nurse completed training on the new medication module?',
 'The EHR training log', ['The EHR audit trail', 'The user access list', 'The help desk ticket log'],
 "Training documentation records who needs training, when it is scheduled and who completed it. It never belongs in a patient's chart."),
('A clinic is rolling out an EHR upgrade. Who is usually trained first to help coworkers at the elbow?',
 'Super users from each department', ['The IT help desk staff', 'Department managers', "The vendor's sales team"],
 'Super users are staff trained early and in depth who then support and coach their colleagues during go-live.'),
('A trainee keeps forgetting how to order a lab panel. Which coaching approach suits an adult learner?',
 'Let her do it while you guide her', ['Give her a printed tip sheet', 'Show her once more on your own screen', 'Have her watch a recorded demo'],
 'Adults learn best by doing a real task with feedback. Doing it for her teaches nothing she can repeat alone.'),
('Staff at a satellite clinic must learn a new feature, but no trainer can travel there. What should a remote session include?',
 'A shared screen and hands-on practice', ['A recorded video sent by email', 'A tip sheet mailed before go-live', 'A conference call with slides and no demo'],
 'Remote training works when learners see the screen, try the steps and ask questions. Paper or one-way messages leave gaps.'),
('Users keep asking the same question about printing visit summaries. What training resource would help most?',
 'A short FAQ or tip sheet', ['A full retraining class', 'A note in the next newsletter', 'A help desk ticket for each user'],
 'Frequently asked questions and one-page tip sheets answer repeated questions quickly and can be added to the EHR reference library.'),
("The vendor's update will change where allergies are entered. What should staff receive before go-live?",
 'Notice of the change and its effects', ["The vendor's full release notes", 'A reminder after the first error is reported', 'The version number and date'],
 'Users need to know what changes, when, and how their workflow shifts, through release notes, huddles or tip sheets, before the update goes live.'),
("Where would an EHR specialist look first for the vendor's instructions on a rarely used screen?",
 'The EHR software reference library', ["The clinic's policy manual", 'The help desk ticket history', "A coworker's saved notes"],
 'The reference library holds user guides, release notes and job aids for the software.'),
('A user reports that the EHR froze. Which basic step should be tried before escalating?',
 'Close the program and log back in', ['Reinstall the EHR software', 'Call the vendor directly', "Restart the clinic's main EHR server"],
 'Simple steps (close and reopen, check connections, restart the device) fix many problems. Anything beyond that is escalated to IT.'),
('When opening a help desk ticket for an EHR error, what information is most useful to include?',
 'A screenshot and computer name', ["The patient's chart contents", "The user's login password", 'A guess at the cause'],
 "A screenshot of the error and the device's name or IP address let IT find and repeat the problem. Passwords are never shared, even with IT."),
('Basic troubleshooting has not fixed a printer that will not print labels. What is the next step?',
 'Escalate it through the IT process', ['Order a replacement printer', 'Handwrite labels until it works', 'Wait to see whether it clears by itself'],
 "When first-line steps fail, the issue is escalated through the facility's IT help desk process rather than worked around."),
("The scheduling system lists a patient's phone number as different from the one in the EHR. What is this?",
 'A data discrepancy', ['A duplicate record', 'A privacy breach', 'A merge request'],
 'Conflicting values for the same field across the EHR and practice management system are discrepancies to identify and reconcile.'),
('Two charts are found for the same patient, each with part of her history. How should this be handled?',
 'Report it for a merge', ['Delete the newer chart', 'Keep both and cross-reference', 'Move the notes over by hand'],
 'Duplicate records are reported to the team that merges them under policy, so no history is lost. Deleting a chart destroys part of the legal record.'),
("A patient's allergy list in the EHR does not match what an outside hospital sent. What should the EHR specialist do?",
 'Flag it for clinical review', ['Replace it with the outside list', 'Add any new allergies yourself', 'Keep the list that is newer'],
 'Conflicting clinical data is flagged for the provider or nurse to reconcile with the patient. A clerical guess could leave out a real allergy.'),
('Before a monthly import of demographic updates, a sample of records is compared with the source. What is this called?',
 'Data validation', ['Data mining', 'Data destruction', 'Data encryption'],
 'Data validation checks that data is accurate and complete, often by comparing samples against the source or running rules on fields.'),
('A field for date of birth will not accept 02/30/1990. What feature is at work?',
 'A data validation rule', ['An audit trail', 'A clinical decision alert', 'A password policy'],
 'Validation rules reject impossible or wrongly formatted entries, such as a date that does not exist.'),
('A new patient asks why she should sign up for the portal. Which benefit is accurate?',
 'She can view results and send messages', ['She can update the diagnoses on her problem list', 'She can order her own lab tests', 'She can see her full billing ledger'],
 'A patient portal lets patients view parts of their record, results and appointments and send secure messages. They cannot edit clinical entries or order tests.'),
('A patient forgot her portal password. What should be done before resetting it?',
 'Verify her identity', ['Unlock her old password', 'Email her a temporary password', 'Confirm her account number'],
 'Identity is verified before any reset so no one else gains access to her record. Passwords are never sent or set to guessable values.'),
("A mother wants portal access to her 6-year-old son's record. What kind of account is usually set up?",
 'Proxy access', ['A shared family login', 'A guest account', 'A staff-assisted account'],
 "Proxy access lets an authorized person, such as a parent of a young child, view a patient's portal record under the organization's policy."),
('A patient cannot find her lab results on the portal. What is the best help?',
 'Show her where results appear', ['Mail her a printed copy', 'Read the results to her by phone', 'Reset her portal account'],
 'Portal support includes navigation help. Results release rules aside, showing the patient where results appear lets her find them next time.'),
('A clinic gives each new portal user a one-time code to link the account to the right chart. What is this code for?',
 'Activating the account', ['Resetting her password', 'Verifying her insurance', 'Paying the visit balance'],
 'An activation (enrollment) code ties the new portal account to the correct patient record after identity is checked.'),
('A records request must be faxed to another clinic. What should go with it?',
 'A confidentiality cover sheet', ["A copy of the patient's ID", 'A signed request from the staff member', 'A billing summary'],
 'A fax cover sheet names the sender and recipient and carries a confidentiality notice in case it reaches the wrong number.'),
('Which is an example of hardware rather than software?',
 'A document scanner', ["The EHR's scheduling module", 'A spreadsheet program', 'The operating system'],
 'Hardware is physical equipment, such as scanners, monitors and signature pads. Programs and operating systems are software.'),
('Which program is best suited to totaling weekly copay collections by day?',
 'A spreadsheet', ['A word processor', 'A web browser', 'An email client'],
 'Spreadsheets hold numbers in rows and columns and calculate totals; word processors are for documents.'),
('Two computers in a clinic share files through the office network. What connects them?',
 'A local area network', ['A dedicated fax line', 'A USB flash drive', 'A barcode reader'],
 'A local area network (LAN) links computers in one location so they can share files, printers and the EHR.'),
("To move a patient's portrait from a webcam into her EHR chart, which step is part of the import?",
 'Attaching it to the correct patient', ['Saving it to a shared folder', "Emailing it to the clinic's shared inbox", 'Printing it to scan later'],
 'Imported images must be attached to the right patient record, often from within the chart, so they are stored securely and not left on the device.'),
('An EHR shows three new hires who still need training on the lab module. Which training record field helps track this?',
 'Who needs training and when', ["Each hire's login name", "Each hire's start date", "Each hire's department"],
 'Training documentation lists who needs training, when it is scheduled and when it was done, so gaps can be closed.'),
('At check-out, which task is usually handled?',
 'Scheduling the follow-up visit', ['Verifying insurance', 'Updating the address', 'Taking vital signs'],
 'Check-out typically collects any payment due, books follow-up visits and gives visit summaries. Vitals and the assessment belong to the clinical visit.'),
("A provider's schedule is set for 15-minute slots, but new patient visits need 30. How is this best handled?",
 'Give that visit type a longer slot', ['Ask new patients to arrive 15 minutes early', "Book new patients at day's end", 'Double-book a 15-minute slot'],
 'Scheduling templates can give each visit type its own length, so new patients get the time they need without overbooking.'),
('An outside urgent care sends a visit summary through a health information exchange. Which task does this support?',
 'Acquiring outside patient data', ['Importing device data', 'Reconciling data discrepancies', 'Generating encounter forms'],
 "Bringing records in from other providers, labs and EHR systems is part of acquiring external data for the patient's chart."),
("A visit note lists a patient's address as an old apartment, while registration shows a new house. Which system holds the record of truth for her address?",
 'The registration demographics', ["The provider's visit note", 'The last claim sent', 'The insurance card on file'],
 'Demographics are kept current at registration and flow to other screens. A note written months ago is not where addresses are maintained.'),
('A clinic upgrades its EHR and moves the print button. Where is the fastest place to tell all staff before the morning clinic?',
 'A short huddle or system message', ['An email after go-live', 'The next staff meeting', 'A note in the EHR training log for each user'],
 "Huddles and system messages reach every user quickly, which suits a small workflow change that affects the day's work."),
]
